# The `tabula::no-static-exit` fixture: a row that cannot be left statically.
#
# `Fault` is [IGNORE, EMIT(Alarm), IGNORE]. An EMIT cell stays in the state it
# is in -- it names an effect, not a destination -- so no cell in that row has
# a static target and none is dynamic either. The machine can enter Fault and,
# as far as the matrix can prove, never leaves.
#
# Not `dead-row`: that fires only when every cell IGNOREs, and the EMIT keeps
# this row alive. The two lints subsume in that order on purpose, so a fixture
# for this one has to have exactly one non-IGNORE cell that goes nowhere.
#
# This is also the first fixture in the suite to use EMIT at all. Every parser
# has supported it since the format existed and none has been asked to.
#
# Quiet elsewhere: `Idle` has a HANDLE and `Blinking` a GO, so neither trips
# this lint; a HANDLE anywhere means the matrix is not fully static, which
# gates off `no-static-entry` -- see effects-never.tbl, which pins that gate
# from the other side; no column is all IGNORE; 5 of 9 IGNORE is 55%; no
# UNREACHABLE cells; no payloads at all, so `payload-hoist` cannot fire.

machine Beacon
initial Idle
states  Idle Blinking Fault
actions Start Pulse Clear

Idle     | HANDLE | IGNORE      | IGNORE
Blinking | IGNORE | EMIT(Flash) | GO(Idle)
Fault    | IGNORE | EMIT(Alarm) | IGNORE
